//! # Target capabilities
//!
//! The documentation header identifies the Hew release whose capability rules
//! apply. Hew has three distinct WebAssembly contexts: browser analysis through
//! `@hew-lang/wasm`, deterministic browser execution in the sandbox VM, and
//! WASI execution built with `--target=wasm32-wasi`. Browser analysis does not
//! by itself establish that a program can execute in either runtime.
//!
//! ## WASI support in this release
//!
//! | Surface | WASI disposition |
//! | --- | --- |
//! | Primitive values, records, enums, patterns, generics, collections | Supported |
//! | Actors, completion calls, supervision, links and monitors | Supported on the cooperative scheduler |
//! | `scope`, tasks, `fork`, `await`, `race`, `select` | Supported |
//! | In-memory streams and semaphores | Supported |
//! | `sleep` and `#[every(duration)]` | Supported with a warning: timing follows cooperative driver ticks |
//! | Host-supplied directory resources | Supported when the host grants explicit directory authority |
//! | Ambient files and file-backed streams | Rejected |
//! | TCP, HTTP, WebSocket, SMTP, TLS, QUIC, and DNS modules | Rejected |
//! | Process execution, OS environment helpers, signals, descriptor readiness | Rejected |
//! | Distributed nodes and remote actors | Rejected |
//! | Secure random bytes, encryption, and signing modules | Rejected |
//!
//! The compiler reports target-specific warnings and rejections before code
//! generation. Treat an unclassified host capability as unsupported even if a
//! particular WASI host happens to expose a similar primitive; Hew's standard
//! library still needs an admitted cross-target implementation.
//!
//! ## Choosing a target
//!
//! Use native builds for operating-system networking, subprocesses, ambient
//! filesystem access, distributed actors, and the native cryptography modules.
//! Use WASI for portable computation and cooperative concurrency when the host
//! can provide the required standard input or explicit directory resources.
//! Use the browser analysis package for editor and compiler services. Use the
//! sandbox VM only for programs whose declared operations are implemented by
//! that VM; its deterministic scheduler is a separate execution contract from
//! WASI and native execution.
